/** @page jnireleasenotesguide jni Module Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section jni_3_0_0 jni Module Version 3.0.0

    - <b>Security</b>: a classpath with a NUL character is now refused with an exception instead of being cut at the
      NUL character, which made it another classpath, both for the sandbox's filesystem policy check and for the
      class loader: the \c add-classpath and \c add-relative-classpath module commands and their \c global-* forms
      raise \c JNI-ERROR, and the \c classpath option of the \c jdbc driver raises \c JDBC-OPTION-ERROR (a %Qore
      library that refuses a NUL character in every DBI option value raises \c DBI-OPTION-ERROR first);
      \c QORE_JNI_CLASSPATH, an environment variable, cannot have a NUL character (qore#6771)
    - fixed \c Jni::org::qore::jni::invoke() and \c invoke_nonvirtual(), which were declared to return
      \c Jni::java::lang::Object, but return the value of the method converted to %Qore, as a direct call of the
      method does, which is often not an object: for example, an \c int, a \c float or a \c bool for a primitive
      type or a boxed value, a \c string for a \c java.lang.String, and \c NOTHING for \c null or a \c void method
      (see @ref jni_java_to_qore); a method call
      made directly on the result (ex: <tt>jni::invoke(m, o).toString()</tt>) crashed the process when the result
      was not an object, and assigning the result to a variable of its type (ex:
      <tt>int len = jni::invoke(m, o);</tt>) was a parse-time type error; they are now declared to return \c auto;
      the values that they return are unchanged (qore#6808)
    - fixed the jni APIs in a Program that has no jni data, a child Program created with
      \c PO_NO_INHERIT_PROGRAM_DATA, or one whose copy of its parent's jni data failed, which has the namespaces of the
      jni module but not its data: \c get_class_loader() crashed the process with a null pointer dereference, as did
      \c define_class(), \c get_byte_code(), \c get_java_name(), \c implement_interface(), \c invoke() and
      \c invoke_nonvirtual(); the Program's jni data, with its own class loader, is now created on demand, as for a jni
      module command issued in the Program; that class loader does not delegate to the parent Program's, so the child
      cannot load classes from the parent's classpath with it (qore#6801)
    - fixed the release of a Program's jni data, which ended the process in \c std::terminate() (exit code 134) when
      the release of the Program's save object callback (see \c set_save_object_callback()) raised a %Qore exception,
      for example in the destructor of an object that only the callback held, or when the object's class belonged to
      the released Program, whose code cannot run any more at that point: the release threw a C++ exception into the
      teardown of the Program; it now never throws, and the exception is reported as an unhandled exception on
      \c stderr; an exception raised when the callback is replaced with \c set_save_object_callback() is now raised
      to its caller instead of being reported as an unhandled exception (qore#6773)
    - fixed \c set_save_object_callback() and the static \c JavaArray::get() method, which ended the process in
      \c std::terminate() (exit code 134) when a JNI error occurred in them (ex: the thread could not be attached to
      the JVM): they now raise it as a %Qore exception, as all other functions and methods of the module do
      (qore#6789)
    - fixed a use-after-free when the save object callback of a Program (see \c set_save_object_callback()) was
      replaced or removed while it ran, by the callback itself or by another thread: the callback ran without a
      reference to it, so replacing it could free it during the call; it now runs through a reference that is
      released when it returns, and an exception raised by that release (ex: in the destructor of an object that only
      the replaced callback held) is raised to the Java code that created the object (qore#6790)
    - fixed the module's teardown and cleanup paths, which ended the process in \c std::terminate() (exit code 134)
      when a step of them failed with a JNI error (ex: stopping the module's native cleanup thread at process exit):
      the module's delete function, its \c atexit() handler, its Program cleanup callback and the rollback of a
      failed module initialization now report the error on \c stderr and continue (qore#6788)
    - fixed the creation of a child Program from a Program that uses the module, which ended the process in
      \c std::terminate() (exit code 134) when the module's data could not be copied for the child Program (ex: the
      thread could not be attached to the JVM): the error is now reported on \c stderr, and the child Program is
      created without the module data of its parent, as the %Qore library offers no way yet to fail the creation of
      the child Program with the error (qore#6787)
    - fixed bare wildcard classpath entries such as \c *.jar being dropped by the class loader. They now load matching
      archives from the JVM's working directory (qore#6740, see @ref jniimport).
    - fixed a C++ exception in a JNI native method of the module, which ended the process in \c std::terminate()
      (exit code 134) instead of being raised in Java: the native methods raised the Java exception of an error with a
      class that they looked up in their catch handlers, and the lookup throws a C++ exception if it fails, and some
      native methods and the helpers that they call had no handler for some C++ exceptions (ex: a call from Java with
      \c QoreJavaApi.callFunction() before the thread was attached to %Qore, or \c QoreObject.className()); every
      native method now raises any C++ exception as a Java exception (a %Qore exception as an
      \c org.qore.jni.QoreExceptionWrapper, \c std::bad_alloc as an \c OutOfMemoryError and any other C++ exception as
      an \c Error), and the classes of these exceptions are looked up when the module is initialized (qore#6739)
    - <b>Incompatible change</b>: a Java string with a NUL character that is used as a %Qore hash key or as a name is
      now refused with an exception instead of being cut at the NUL character, silently, which made it another key
      or name (qore#6738, see @ref jni_strings):
      - a Java \c Map key or JDBC column label with a NUL character raises \c HASH-KEY-ERROR; it was cut at the NUL
        character, so that two keys that differ only after it became one key, and one value replaced the other
      - a class, package, module, method, field, function, member or namespace name with a NUL character raises
        \c JNI-ERROR: given to \c load_class(), \c define_class(), \c get_byte_code(), \c get_java_name() or a
        module command (\c import, \c define-class, \c define-pending-class, \c register-shared-class,
        \c mark-module-injected), as the name of a Java class, method or field that a %Qore class is made for, as a
        name that Java code passes to %Qore (\c QoreJavaApi and \c QoreObject calls), or as the %Qore name of a
        class that Java loads; for example, \c load_class() of <tt>"java/lang/String\x00junk"</tt> returned
        \c java.lang.String, and Java method <tt>"me\x00th"</tt> became %Qore method \c me(); a parameter, return
        value or field whose class has a NUL character in its name is declared as any object
      - an \c org.qore.jni.QoreException whose argument is a \c Map with a key that has a NUL character is reported
        as the Java exception that it is, as for any other argument that cannot be converted
      - fixed the values already converted for a Java \c Map or \c List, which leaked when the conversion of a nested
        value raised an error
      - fixed an error raised while converting the arguments of a call from Java to a %Qore function, static method
        or constructor (\c QoreJavaApi.callFunction(), \c QoreJavaApi.callStaticMethod(),
        \c QoreJavaApi.newObjectSave(); ex: a Java exception thrown by a \c Map argument, or a \c Map key with a NUL
        character), which aborted the process instead of being raised in Java, as no C++ exception may leave a JNI
        native method
    - fixed the \c import module command, as issued with \c Program::issueModuleCmd(), for a Program that has code
      pending (parsed with \c Program::parsePending() and not yet committed), as when Qorus initializes Java in a
      container: with %Qore's consistent thread-local Program data (qore#6185), an import of a class failed with
      <tt>PROGRAM-ERROR: the Program accessed is currently undergoing parsing and cannot be accessed at runtime</tt>,
      as the module entered the Program at runtime to add the class to it; adding a class changes the Program's
      definitions under its parse lock and runs none of its code, so the Program is no longer entered when it is the
      current Program (qore#6758)
    - fixed the \c define-class, \c define-pending-class and \c import module commands for a Java class that extends
      a %Qore class (ex: the Java classes of Qorus's new API, which extend \c qore.OMQ.UserApi.* classes), as issued
      with \c Program::issueModuleCmd() for a Program that has code pending: with %Qore's consistent thread-local
      Program data (qore#6185), the command failed with <tt>PROGRAM-ERROR: the Program accessed is currently
      undergoing parsing and cannot be accessed at runtime</tt> when the byte code of the Java class of the %Qore
      superclass was generated for the Program (the first time it was needed in the process), as the module entered
      the Program at runtime for it; generating byte code reads the Program's definitions under its parse lock and
      runs none of its code, so the Program is no longer entered for it when it is the current Program, and the same
      applies to listing the classes of a namespace for the Java compiler, which now reads the namespace under the
      Program's parse lock (qore#6785)
    - fixed strings that cross between %Qore and Java outside the conversion of values: they are now converted
      strictly to and from UTF-8 with their lengths, as values are (qore#6604, see @ref jni_strings):
      - the messages of Java exceptions that the module raises with the text of a %Qore exception, and the message of
        a %Qore exception read in Java (\c QoreExceptionWrapper.getMessage()), were cut at a NUL character, a code
        or description in another encoding than UTF-8 was not converted, and a character outside the Basic
        Multilingual Plane was garbled (ex: in the error of a Java class that cannot be generated for a %Qore class);
        JNI's \c ThrowNew(), which takes modified UTF-8, is no longer used for a message that is not ASCII
      - the description of an \c org.qore.jni.QoreException thrown in Java was cut at a NUL character, and its code
        was not converted to the default encoding
      - in the \c jdbc driver, a string bind value and SQL were cut at a NUL character, a bind value and the \c url
        option in another encoding than UTF-8 were not converted, nor were the user, password and database name of a
        datasource with a default encoding other than UTF-8, and with such a default encoding, column labels were
        hash keys in UTF-8 and the metadata names (\c getServerVersion(), \c getClientVersion(),
        \c getDriverRealName()) were not marked as UTF-8
      - a Kotlin script given to \c kotlin_eval() was cut at a NUL character and was not converted from another
        encoding than UTF-8; the arguments of the \c kotlin-eval, \c set-property and other module commands were cut
        at a NUL character, and with a default encoding other than UTF-8, they were not converted
      - with a default encoding other than UTF-8, a hash key converted to a Java \c Map key, and a \c Map key
        converted to a hash key, were not converted
      - a class name with a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane given to \c define_class() or
        \c load_class() was passed to JNI's \c DefineClass() or \c FindClass() in standard UTF-8 instead of the
        modified UTF-8 that they take, which failed
      - the output of \c JavaArray::deepToString() was cut at a NUL character
      - <b>Security</b>: a classpath (the \c add-classpath and \c add-relative-classpath module commands, their
        \c global-* forms, the \c jdbc driver's \c classpath option and \c QORE_JNI_CLASSPATH) is now converted once
        from the default encoding to the Java string that the class loader uses, and the sandbox's filesystem policy
        checks each entry as the JVM uses it: as an absolute path resolved as the JVM resolves it, in the bytes of the
        JVM's file name encoding; with a default encoding other than UTF-8, an entry with a non-ASCII character was
        checked in another representation than the one that was used, so it could be refused although allowed, or
        not be usable, and a relative entry was checked against the current directory of the process instead of the
        directory that the JVM resolves it against; under a sandbox, an entry that the JVM's file name encoding cannot
        represent exactly is refused with \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR.  The policy checks the classpath entries; the files
        that the class loader reads under a directory entry or that a wildcard entry matches are not checked one by
        one
      - <b>Incompatible change</b>: a string that cannot be converted to UTF-8 from its encoding now raises an
        exception instead of being passed to Java with its bytes as they are (except in the message of an exception,
        whose invalid sequences become U+FFFD), and with a default encoding other than UTF-8, a Java \c Map key or
        JDBC column label that the default encoding cannot represent now raises \c ENCODING-CONVERSION-ERROR instead
        of becoming a hash key in UTF-8
    - fixed a module that Java's loading of a \c qoremod.* class could not load: its error was raised in Java, and
      also printed as an unhandled %Qore exception
    - fixed %Qore handlers for \c SIGUSR2 never running while the JVM was loaded: HotSpot suspends and resumes its
      threads with \c SIGUSR2 by default and handles it in its own threads; the module now gives the JVM that it
      creates a free real-time signal in \c _JAVA_SR_SIGNUM (only while the JVM is created, and only if the variable
      is not set), so \c SIGUSR2 handlers run, and reserves the signal that the JVM uses as it does the other signals
      that the JVM needs; this changes which signals %Qore code can handle while the module is loaded: the chosen
      real-time signal (normally \c SIGRTMAX) cannot be handled, the module cannot be loaded if no real-time signal is
      free, and \c SIGUSR2, whose handlers never ran, cannot be handled on platforms without real-time signals that the
      JVM accepts (such as macOS) and when a Java process has created the JVM (qore#6707, see @ref jni_sr_signal)
    - fixed Java's \c System.exit() and \c Runtime.halt() aborting the process
      (<tt>external native threads still active while stopping the cleanup worker</tt>) when a \c ThreadPool worker
      was active: the JVM called C \c exit() on its VM Thread, which ran libqore's static destructors while %Qore
      threads were still running; the JVM now ends the process through %Qore's process exit (as the %Qore \c exit()
      function does) with the status given, after Java's shutdown hooks have run for \c System.exit(), also when the
      call is made in a Java thread that %Qore did not create (qore#6698, see @ref jni_process_exit)
    - fixed the handling of \c SIGTERM, \c SIGINT and \c SIGHUP in a process with \c libjsig preloaded or linked in:
      the JVM was started without reduced signals (<tt>-Xrs</tt>) in that case, so it handled these signals itself;
      %Qore signal handlers were not called, and the JVM ended the process with \c System.exit(), which called C
      \c exit() on the JVM's VM Thread while %Qore threads were still running, so the process aborted
      (<tt>external native threads still active while stopping the cleanup worker</tt>) when a \c ThreadPool worker
      was active; the JVM is now always started with reduced signals, so %Qore owns the asynchronous signals and the
      JVM still handles \c SIGSEGV, \c SIGBUS, \c SIGFPE and \c SIGILL, which \c libjsig still chains with the handlers
      of other runtimes; in this case, Java shutdown hooks no longer run when the process gets a shutdown signal
      (they run when the JVM is destroyed at the normal end of the process), and \c SIGQUIT no longer prints a Java
      thread dump (use <tt>jcmd <i>pid</i> Thread.print</tt>); <tt>-XX:-ReduceSignalUsage</tt> in
      \c QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS no longer turns reduced signals off (see @ref jni_signals)
    - fixed Java code that \c Jni::org::qore::jni::invoke(), \c invoke_nonvirtual(), \c kotlin_eval(),
      \c JavaArray::toString() and \c JavaArray::deepToString() run (also the static initializers that the lookup of
      the method of \c invoke() runs, and the discovery of the Kotlin script engine by these functions,
      \c kotlin_scripting_available(), \c kotlin_scripting_retry() and the \c kotlin-eval module command, which
      failed for the caller when only its class loader had the scripting jars), the static initializers that the
      lookup of a Java class and the creation of its %Qore class run (also for a class of the JVM's system class
      path), and Java code that converting the value of a Java field or of a \c JavaArray element runs (ex: the
      methods of a \c Map or a \c List), calling into %Qore through the Program of an earlier Java call made for
      another Program instead of the calling code's Program (the Program that the class is created in, for a static
      initializer): these now set the thread's Java context to that Program's context (a read of a value only when
      converting it runs Java code that the value's class can override, so that other reads cost no further call into
      Java); and a call into %Qore from a Java class generated from a %Qore class, and the loading of a \c qore.* or
      \c qoremod.* class and the listing of the classes of such a package
      (\c QoreURLClassLoader.getClassesInNamespace()), either of which can load a %Qore module whose initialization
      code makes Java calls for other Programs, now save the thread's Java context and restore it when they return to
      Java, as the other calls from Java into %Qore do (qore#6551, see @ref jni_callback_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: with a %Qore library that checks the destinations of connections against the sandbox of the
      code that chose them (qore#5747), the \c jdbc driver, which cannot report its destinations, is refused for a
      connection chosen by code (the code that created the datasource or set its database name or connection
      values) whose governing sandbox restricts network or filesystem access, whatever the sandbox trusts
      (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - fixed Java code using the thread's Java context (the Program that Java's calls into %Qore on the thread, such
      as \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosure.call(), resolve) that a Java call made for another Program had left: Java code
      that called into %Qore, and Java code run by a Java object used as a %Qore closure, then called into %Qore
      through that other Program (running its entry handler, and its sandbox applying to the code called); a call from
      Java into %Qore through \c QoreClosure, \c QoreObject, \c QoreJavaApi or a proxy's \c QoreInvocationHandler now
      saves the context and the thread's context class loader and restores them when it returns to Java, also when an
      exception is raised, and a Java object used as a %Qore closure sets the context (and the thread's context class
      loader) for the Java code it runs, as Java method calls do, which costs one more JNI call per closure call; a
      static method uses the jni context of the Program that %Qore runs it in (the class's source Program, or the
      caller's Program once the source Program has released the class), instead of reading the data of the class's
      namespace Program, which the thread is not necessarily in; and a Java call whose Program is deleted by another
      thread just before the call enters it is now made in the context of the calling code, as for a Program deleted
      before the call, instead of raising \c PROGRAM-ERROR (a Java callback of such a Program is still refused with
      \c PROGRAM-ERROR, as its %Qore code belongs to the deleted Program) (qore#6485, see @ref jni_callback_sandbox)
    - fixed a crash calling a Java object used as a %Qore closure (an object implementing
      \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosureMarker) after the Program that it was returned to had been deleted: the closure kept
      an unreferenced pointer to the Program; it now keeps the Program allocated, and is called in the context of the
      calling code once the Program has been deleted
    - fixed a race between a Java instance method call, or a Java callback (\c QoreInvocationHandler), and the
      deletion of the Program in another thread: the Program's jni data was looked up before the Program was entered,
      and the Program's teardown could destroy it in between; the Program is now entered first, and a Java object used
      as a %Qore closure enters the Program that it was returned to in the same way when another Program calls it,
      which runs that Program's entry handler and raises \c PROGRAM-ERROR if the Program is being torn down or is
      being parsed by another thread (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: the functions that load, define or run Java code or return the class loader
      (\c Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_eval(), \c get_byte_code(), \c load_class(), \c define_class(),
      \c implement_interface(), \c invoke(), \c invoke_nonvirtual() and \c get_class_loader()), and
      \c JavaArray::toString() and \c JavaArray::deepToString(), which run the \c toString() methods of the
      elements, are uncontrolled APIs (\c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API), as the Java classes that they give access to are, so
      a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS cannot call them, nor, with a Qore that refuses uncontrolled APIs at run
      time, code governed by a sandbox that does not allow them; \c check_network_bind(), which only checks the sandbox
      policy, is not

    - <b>Security</b>: built for module API 2.3, the module sets \c cmd_caller_aware and judges every module command
      first by the restrictions of the code that issues it (\c QoreModuleCommandContext, qore#5699): sandboxed
      issuers are refused the commands that load or define Java code, change a classpath or change state that every
      Program shares, and issuers with a restricting parse option the commands that the option forbids
      (\c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR); the exported functions used by the \c python module's Java loader apply the rule of
      \c import, and the \c classpath option of the \c jdbc database driver the rule of \c add-classpath, also with
      an older module API; with module API 2.3 the code of a library module that sets the \c jdbc option, or that
      reaches the \c python module's Java loader, is judged by its sandbox only (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: with a %Qore that has the uncontrolled-API policy of sandboxes (qore#5741), the module command
      rules, the exported import functions and the \c jdbc \c classpath option refuse sandboxed code only when its
      sandbox does not allow uncontrolled APIs (\c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy()); the same holds for
      \c define-class of a class of another Program (see @ref jni_sandbox); the module hash reports this with
      \c "uncontrolled_api_policy" in its \c "info" key
    - <b>Security</b>: with a %Qore that records the code that sets each datasource option
      (\c _QORE_HAS_DATASOURCE_OPTION_RESTRICTIONS, qore#5741), the \c jdbc driver's \c classpath option is judged by
      the code that set it (with \c Datasource::setOption(), a constructor or connection string, or the code that
      created a \c DatasourcePool), not by the code that opens the connection: trusted code that opens a datasource
      whose classpath sandboxed code set is refused it, the option of a trusted pool that grows on a restricted
      caller's thread is not refused (the driver does not connect at all for code that a sandbox that does not allow
      uncontrolled APIs governs, which refuses such a connection with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED first), and the
      classpath entries are checked against the filesystem policy of the code that set the option;
      the module hash reports this with \c "jdbc_option_setter" in its \c "info" key (see @ref jdbc_option_classpath);
      the jars are added to the class loader of the %Program that the datasource or pool object belongs to, not of
      the code that opens the connection; a value set on an open connection is judged by its setter also when it
      equals the current classpath
    - <b>Security</b>: the exported import functions used by the \c python module's Java loader refuse an import into
      a %Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS whoever calls the loader, in addition to judging the code that
      imports (also with module API 2.3)

    - <b>Security</b>: sandboxed code, and a Program with \c PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS, cannot use the module commands
      that change state of the JVM that every Program shares or that run Java code (\c global-add-classpath,
      \c global-add-relative-classpath, \c set-property, \c register-shared-class, \c kotlin-eval;
      \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR); \c define-class is refused in such a Program and to a sandboxed Program for a class of
      another Program; the entries that a sandboxed Program adds with \c add-classpath, \c add-relative-classpath or
      the \c jdbc driver's \c classpath option are checked against its filesystem policy (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: the connection and data provider classes of the apps with \c external_io refuse sandboxed code
      in their constructors (\c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED), also when it creates them directly
    - <b>Security</b>: the Avro, Email, Excel, ICS, ODP, ODS, ODT, PowerPoint, Tika, VCF, Visio and Word data provider
      apps declare \c external_io as well, and their data provider classes refuse sandboxed code in their
      constructors (\c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED): a governing sandbox refuses the uncontrolled APIs
      (\c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API) that their Java calls are, also when library code makes them for a sandboxed caller
      (qore#5741), so they refuse sandboxed callers cleanly up front instead of failing in the middle of a call; the
      provider modules require \c DataProvider 3.12 or later (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: the Access, Busylight, Camel, Flyway, JakartaJMS, JasperReports, JdbcDP, Kafka, MQTT and
      OpcUA data provider apps declare \c external_io: their Java code does network or filesystem I/O that the sandbox
      does not control, so data provider consumers refuse them to sandboxed callers (\c APP-SANDBOX-ACCESS-DENIED);
      the provider modules require \c DataProvider 3.12 or later (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - <b>Security</b>: fixed a call of an instance method of a Java object that another Program owns, which switched
      only the thread's current Program to the object's Program instead of entering it: the caller was hidden from
      the sandbox for the rest of the Java call, so that %Qore code that Java called back during the call, and
      \c org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox queries and captures made by the Java code, saw no sandbox when sandboxed code that
      may use uncontrolled APIs (\c SandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy()) made the call (qore#6183); and %Qore
      code that Java called back ran with the thread-local data of the calling Program, so that a top-level variable
      of the object's Program read as \c NOTHING and a write to it was stored in the caller's data (qore#6185); the
      call now enters the object's Program, which also runs the Program's entry handler once around the Java call
      and holds the Program's thread count for the call, and raises \c PROGRAM-ERROR before the Java method is
      called if the Program is being torn down or is being parsed by another thread (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox);
      a call made in the object's own Program does not enter it again

    - <b>Security</b>: the \c jdbc database driver raises \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED when it connects for code that a
      sandbox network policy restricts (a default network policy that denies, or any allowed or denied IP range or
      allowed port rule; a sandbox manager that restricts nothing does not refuse the driver), because the sandbox network policy cannot be applied to the destinations that a JDBC
      driver resolves and discovers in Java; with a %Qore library that has the sandbox trust API
      (\c QoreSandboxManager::trustsUncontrolledApis(); jni module info key \c sandbox_restricts_api), it is refused
      for code that its governing sandbox does not trust with uncontrolled APIs, whatever the sandbox restricts (every
      member of a composition of sandboxes must trust the code), because the driver is uncontrolled Java code whose
      other I/O, threads, memory and JVM-wide changes the sandbox does not control either (see @ref jni_sandbox)

    - <b>Security</b>: the \c url option and the database name of a \c jdbc datasource, which choose the destination
      and can carry driver properties, are judged by the code that set them: trusted code that opens a datasource
      whose url sandboxed code set is refused with \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR (with a %Qore library that records the
      setters of datasource options); the database name with a %Qore library that records the code that chose a
      connection (see @ref jdbc_option_url)

    - <b>Security</b>: \c Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_available(),
      \c Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_retry(), \c Jni::org::qore::jni::new_array(),
      \c Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray::getClass(), the static \c Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray::get() for a
      binary and the constructor of \c Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler are uncontrolled APIs
      (\c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API), as they initialize Java classes, allocate Java memory or return Java classes

    - <b>Security</b>: the options of the JDBC, Jakarta JMS, Kafka, Camel, JasperReports and Flyway providers that
      name a Java class to load, a classpath to add, or code that Java compiles are refused with
      \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR to sandboxed code; added \c Jni::org::qore::jni::check_class_option() for modules;
      with the sandbox trust API, to code that its governing sandbox (resolved for a policy check) does not trust with
      uncontrolled APIs, whatever the sandbox restricts, as for the \c jdbc driver (see @ref jni_sandbox)

    - <b>Security</b>: a Kafka \c sasl.kerberos.kinit.cmd option other than the default, which names a command that
      the Kafka client runs, is refused with \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR to sandboxed code (see @ref jni_sandbox)

    - fixed calling Java varargs methods with the variable arguments as separate arguments (ex:
      <tt>m.invoke(NOTHING, a, b)</tt> for \c java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(), <tt>String::format("%s %s", a,
      b)</tt>, <tt>Arrays::asList(a, b, c)</tt>): with \c %%strict-args (in \c %%modern) more than one of them
      matched no variant, and otherwise the first was passed as an array nested in the array (\c [[a], \c b]), which
      Java rejected with \c "argument type mismatch" or used as the wrong value; a varargs method now has a variant
      taking the variable arguments as separate arguments, which are packed into the array as in Java, and its
      variant taking the array declares it as a <tt>*list&lt;T&gt;</tt> instead of a <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt>, as
      a single value is a separate variable argument; a single Java array of the array's class is the array, and a
      Java method with fixed parameters for the same number of arguments, also of a parent class, is called
      instead, as in Java; an abstract varargs method (ex: \c java.nio.file.FileSystem.getPath()) has a concrete
      variant for the variable arguments as separate arguments, and keeps the <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt> that a
      %Qore class implementing it declares; a %Qore method overriding a varargs method of a Java interface may
      declare the array as <tt>*list&lt;T&gt;</tt> or <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt> (see @ref jni_array_varargs);
      <b>signature change:</b> the %Qore signatures of Java varargs methods and constructors changed (the array is
      <tt>*list&lt;T&gt;</tt>, and variants for separate arguments and for a
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::JavaArray "JavaArray" were added), so AOT-compiled programs and generated
      documentation that captured the former <tt>softlist&lt;T&gt;</tt> signatures differ and must be rebuilt;
      remaining limitations: a default method of a Java interface, and the variant for separate arguments of an
      abstract method, cannot be called on an object of a %Qore class implementing the interface, the elements of a
      \c byte... array cannot be given as a list, a \c JavaArray is not taken for the typed array of an abstract
      method, only one variant per method name takes a \c JavaArray, and a parent's method with fixed parameters of
      other %Qore types (ex: \c A.m(CharSequence) for \c B.m(String...)) is not preferred as Java does
    - <b>Security</b>: added @ref org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox "org.qore.jni.QoreSandbox", which applies the sandbox
      network and filesystem policy of the %Qore code that called into Java to the I/O that Java code makes itself,
      which %Qore does not see: \c checkNetworkAccess() resolves a host name once with the %Qore resolver, checks
      every address with the policy of %Qore sockets, raises \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED naming a denied address, and
      returns the checked addresses to connect to; \c checkFileAccess() checks a path with the policy of %Qore file
      operations; \c capture() captures the policy for work that Java runs on its own threads, and a check on a Java
      thread with no %Qore caller and no captured policy raises \c JNI-SANDBOX-ERROR instead of allowing the
      operation (see @ref jni_sandbox)
    - fixed the conversion of strings with characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane (ex: emoji) between %Qore
      and Java, which garbled them in both directions: \c "😀" became \c "ð" in Java, and a Java string with one
      became invalid UTF-8 in %Qore, including in the messages of Java exceptions; such a character is a 4-byte
      UTF-8 sequence in %Qore and a surrogate pair in Java; a NUL character is converted in both directions, and
      invalid UTF-8 in a %Qore string (an invalid byte, a truncated or overlong sequence, an encoded surrogate) is
      U+FFFD in Java
    - fixed the \c jdbc driver returning Oracle's \c TIMESTAMP, \c TIMESTAMP \c WITH \c TIME \c ZONE,
      \c TIMESTAMP \c WITH \c LOCAL \c TIME \c ZONE and \c ROWID values as Java objects of Oracle's driver
      (\c oracle.sql.TIMESTAMP and the like), and \c describe() giving these columns as objects: they are dates and
      strings (see @ref jdbc_column_values)
    - the \c jdbc driver returns the value of a \c BLOB, \c CLOB or \c NCLOB as binary or a string, as the native
      %Qore drivers do, and \c describe() gives these columns as binary and strings; the value is read in chunks,
      which a cancelled thread or an interrupted program stops (see @ref jdbc_column_values); <b>behaviour
      change:</b> they were returned as \c java.sql.Blob and \c java.sql.Clob objects, which code that read them as
      Java objects must no longer do; the whole value is read into memory
    - added the \c jdbc driver's \c lob-max-size option: the most bytes or characters read from a large object whose
      length the driver does not give (see @ref jdbc_option_lob_max_size)
    - fixed the \c jdbc driver dropping the fraction of a second of \c TIME values (ex: SQL Server's \c time(7)) and
      the offset of \c TIME \c WITH \c TIME \c ZONE values, and returning PostgreSQL's \c 'infinity' as a date in
      the year 292278994: it is the date that the \c pgsql driver returns (see @ref jdbc_column_values)
    - fixed a crash of the \c jdbc driver (an exception that terminated the process) when a cancelled thread used a
      connection or closed a statement
    - fixed a %Qore Program destroyed on a cancelled thread leaving its Java class loader with a pointer to the
      destroyed Program
    - fixed the \c jdbc driver binding an integer of -128 to 127 as a \c TINYINT and one of -32768 to 32767 as a
      \c SMALLINT: SQL Server stores a \c TINYINT unsigned, so a negative value was stored as another one (-1 as
      255) when a statement prepared with a small positive value was run again; an integer is bound as an \c INTEGER
      or a \c BIGINT
    - the \c jdbc driver's \c execRaw() executes the SQL with a plain \c java.sql.Statement instead of a prepared
      statement, as the other DBI drivers do:
      - the SQL is sent to the server as given: it is no longer parsed for placeholders, so \c %v, \c %d and \c %s
        are not replaced, \c "\%" and \c "\:" keep their backslash, and a \c % outside a string that does not follow a
        letter or a digit (ex: SQL Server's and PostgreSQL's modulo operator in <tt>7 % 4</tt>) no longer raises
        \c JDBC-PARSE-ERROR
      - on SQL Server, the effects of the SQL last for the session: a \c \#temp table it creates can be used by later
        statements (it was dropped when the statement ended, as SQL Server runs a prepared statement with
        \c sp_prepexec), and a \c SET option it sets stays set
    - fixed a deadlock when a module failed to load while another thread generated the Java class of a %Qore class:
      once the two threads had deadlocked, every thread loading a module or generating a Java class waited forever
    - <b>Security</b>: %Qore code that Java calls on a Java thread - a
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" proxy, a closure passed to Java, or a
      method of a %Qore object passed to Java - now runs under the sandbox of the code that handed it to Java, and is
      released or destroyed under it; previously it ran with no sandbox on a Java thread that %Qore did not start
      (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox)
    - fixed the \c "compat-types" module option being enabled by any value: it is enabled by a value that is
      @ref True in a boolean context, so @ref False and \c NULL leave it disabled (see @ref jni_compatguide)
    - fixed a crash when a %Qore callback on a Java thread made a Java call that called back into %Qore on the same
      thread: the inner callback detached the thread from %Qore while the outer one was still running
    - fixed a data race when a child Program was created while another thread imported a Java class into its
      parent: the child copied the parent's class map without the lock that protects it
    - <b>Security</b>: fixed a Java class imported into one Program becoming visible in every Program that loaded the
      jni module afterwards, but not in the Programs that had loaded it before, so that code naming a Java class it
      never imported worked or failed depending on what other Programs had imported first, and a sandboxed Program
      could see classes imported by other Programs; an import is now visible only in the Program that made it and in
      the child Programs that inherit its classes, and code must import every Java class it names outside
      \c java.lang.* (see @ref jniimport)
    - fixed reading a Java instance field of an object created with a %Qore constructor returning no value instead
      of the field's value; reading or assigning a Java instance field now reads or writes the field of the Java
      object for every object, and an assignment in %Qore is visible in Java (see @ref jni_class_fields)
    - the declared types of Java return values, fields, static fields and constants of reference types (objects,
      including \c java.lang.String and the boxed types, and arrays) accept no value, as a Java reference can be
      \c null (ex: \c *string for a \c String return value, <tt>*softlist&lt;int&gt;</tt> for an \c int[] field, so
      that a \c null array is no value instead of an empty list), and accept the value the conversion yields (ex:
      \c *int for a \c java.lang.Character, \c auto for a \c java.lang.Number or \c java.lang.CharSequence, a list or
      the Java object for a list return value); this fixes \c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR when reading a \c null field and
      parse-time type errors when assigning a return value to the type it has (see @ref jni_declared_value_types)
    - <b>Migration</b>: the declared types of Java parameters of reference types accept no value, which is passed to
      Java as \c null (ex: \c *string for a \c java.lang.String parameter), and a %Qore argument selects the variant
      Java would select for the value it is converted to (see @ref jni_declared_param_types):
      - a %Qore class implementing or overriding a Java method must declare the new parameter types (ex:
        <tt>messageArrived(*string topic, ...)</tt> instead of <tt>messageArrived(string topic, ...)</tt>), or the
        class has unimplemented abstract variants (\c ABSTRACT-CLASS-ERROR)
      - a string, a list, a binary or another converted value now selects the variant of the more specific class:
        for example a string now calls <tt>m(CharSequence)</tt> instead of <tt>m(Object)</tt>, and a string, a list
        or a binary now calls <tt>m(java.io.Serializable)</tt> instead of <tt>m(Object)</tt>
      - no value passed for an array parameter is \c null instead of an empty array; the variable arguments of a
        varargs method are still an empty array when none are given
      - a parameter of a class that converted values implement or extend (ex: \c java.lang.CharSequence,
        \c java.lang.Comparable, \c java.time.temporal.Temporal, \c java.lang.Iterable) takes the %Qore value (ex:
        a string for a \c CharSequence), which it rejected before
      - a call passing no value for overloads that all take it selects the same variant at parse time and at run
        time, which needs %Qore 3.0 with the fix for such calls
    - fixed a Java method call ending the process when its thread could not be attached to the JVM, as when the
      thread's cancellation was pending; the call now raises the exception (ex: \c THREAD-CANCELLED)
    - fixed the process crashing at exit (\c SIGSEGV, then the JVM aborted) when a destructor raised an exception as
      the module's global context %Qore Program was deleted (ex: a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception); the exception is now
      reported as an unhandled exception at teardown, which makes the exit status non-zero
    - fixed the process crashing after the module failed to initialize after the JVM was started (ex: when the JVM
      lacks a Java module that the jni module needs); a failed initialization now releases everything it set up,
      including the JVM, and reports \c MODULE-INIT-ERROR
    - fixed a Java \c static \c final field holding \c null being mirrored by a class constant with the value \c 0; it
      is now a constant with no value, declared with the field's type (ex: \c *string for a \c java.lang.String
      field)
    - fixed a child process created with \c fork() after the JVM was started waiting forever when it exited or ended
      its script, or when it deleted a Program whose %Qore code a Java thread was running; Java cannot be used in
      such a child, and a use of Java there now raises \c JNI-FORK-ERROR (see @ref jni_fork)
    - fixed converting a list of binary values, closures or call references to Java where the Java type is not
      given (for example for a Java \c Object parameter, or for a %Qore class constant in the Java class generated
      for the class): such a list now becomes an array of byte arrays (\c byte[][]) or of
      \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosure objects; the conversion raised \c JNI-ERROR ("cannot create a Java array from a
      list that contains values of type 'binary'"), and defining the Java class of a %Qore class with such a
      constant failed with \c java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError
    - fixed deep recursion on the primary thread after the JVM was created overflowing the stack instead of raising
      \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED: the primary thread's stack limit was set for the default thread stack size, from the
      position of the stack when %Qore started, without the primary thread's extra stack guard and without the zones
      that the JVM protects at the end of the stack; it is now set to the end of the stack as the JVM sees it (see
      @ref jni_primary_thread_stack)
    - made Java socket-based JDBC connection establishment cancellable using a joined virtual thread
    - fixed a native abort while converting a Java exception with Qore cancellation pending
    - closed connections after failed metadata/session initialization and cancellation races


    @anchor jni_2_5_0
    @anchor jni_2_6_0
    @anchor jni_2_7_0

    @subsection jni_3_0_0_overview Overview

    JNI 3.0 brings Kotlin language integration, new data providers, columnar JDBC results, and improved
    Java API generation to %Qore 3.0. It requires <b>%Qore 3.0 and Java 21 or later</b>.

    When upgrading:
    - <b>Update Java imports for module APIs.</b> Module-owned classes now use only
      <tt>qoremod.&lt;module&gt;.&lt;class-path&gt;</tt>; the legacy
      <tt>qore.&lt;module&gt;.&lt;class-path&gt;</tt> aliases are no longer accepted. Recompile affected Java
      sources and regenerate API classes created by older JNI versions. See
      @ref jni_dynamic_import_migration "Java import migration" for examples and the
      \c qjava-migrate-imports helper.
    - <b>Review collection types at the language boundary.</b> Generated Java signatures for
      <tt>hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> parameters now use \c java.util.Map. Java and Kotlin collections convert
      to the most specific %Qore list or hash type, which can restrict later insertion of values with
      other types. See @ref jni_java_to_qore "Java to Qore type conversions".
    - <b>Review code using Java lists and maps as Java objects.</b> The %Qore types declared for Java methods and
      fields now match the values they return: every Java list, such as \c ArrayList or \c LinkedList, is declared
      as <tt>*list&lt;auto&gt;</tt>, and a Java map as <tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> only when its key type is \c String; any other map is
      declared as \c auto, as it is a hash or a Java object depending on its keys (a method returning
      <tt>TreeMap&lt;Integer, String&gt;</tt> still returns a Java object, and is now declared as \c auto).  Code
      that assigns a returned \c ArrayList to a variable declared as the Java class, or that overrides such a method
      with a Java object return type, now fails when it is parsed; use a list instead.  A raw \c java.util.Map return
      value, declared as <tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> before, is declared as \c auto; this only changes parse-time type checks.
      See @ref jni_collection_types "declared types of Java lists and maps".
    - <b>Review code using Java date and time values.</b> The values of \c java.time.OffsetDateTime,
      \c java.time.OffsetTime, \c java.time.LocalDate and \c java.time.LocalTime are now %Qore dates, as those of the
      other Java date and time classes were; code calling Java methods on them must use %Qore date operations
      instead.  The members of the Java classes whose values are dates, also \c java.time.LocalDateTime,
      \c java.time.Instant, \c java.sql.Timestamp, \c java.sql.Date and \c java.sql.Time, are declared with the
      \c date type: a method of one of these types returned a date already but was declared with the Java class, so
      that a variable declared with the Java class for its value no longer parses (it could only hold \c NOTHING);
      a parameter of one of them takes a date, as well as the Java object.  See
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes".
    - <b>Recompile ahead-of-time compiled code with %Qore classes that inherit Java classes.</b> The types of
      private Java members are now declared as \c auto (see @ref jni_member_types "declared types of Java members"),
      and the members of the Java date and time classes as dates (see
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes"), which changes the signatures of the %Qore classes
      created for Java classes, and a %Qore class's signature includes those of its parent classes.  Recompile code
      compiled with \c qcc that defines %Qore classes inheriting Java classes with this version; otherwise its
      classes do not match the same classes parsed from source.
    - <b>Review code using the keys of duplicate columns in \c jdbc results.</b> A duplicate column name is the
      lower-case name with a suffix, as for module-odbc and module-sybase: the columns \c ID and \c ID of a join are
      returned as \c id and \c id_1, and no longer as \c id and \c ID_1 (the JDBC drivers of Oracle and SQL Server
      return the names as written, often in upper case).
    - <b>Update handlers of the \c jdbc driver's lost-transaction error.</b> When the connection is lost in a
      transaction, the \c jdbc DBI driver raises \c JDBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR; it raised \c ODBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR
      before.  Code that catches the old error code must catch the new one.  See
      @ref jdbc_lost_connections "lost connections".
    - <b>Review code executing SQL with several results through the \c jdbc driver.</b> \c exec(),
      \c execRaw() and \c select() return every result of such SQL, and the shape of the value depends on the
      results; \c selectRow(), \c selectRows(), \c selectColumnar() and \c SQLStatement raise an exception
      for more than one result set.  See @ref jdbc_multiple_results "statements with several results".
    - <b>Use explicit lifecycle APIs where deterministic cleanup matters.</b> Native wrappers use
      automatic native-reference cleanup instead of Java finalizers; explicit release remains available. See
      @ref jni_qore_object_lifecycle_managementguide "object lifecycle management" and
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.release().

    @subsection jni_3_0_0_features New Features

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_kotlin Kotlin Integration

    - Compile Kotlin code that uses %Qore APIs with \c qkotlinc, and use the \c kotlin.* and
      \c kotlinmod.* dynamic import prefixes. Kotlin synthetic members, bridge methods, default-parameter
      variants, and companion-object classes are filtered from imported APIs. See the
      @ref jni_kotlin_example "Kotlin example" and @ref jni_dynamic_imports_compile "compilation guide".
    - Evaluate Kotlin scripts at runtime with @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_eval() "kotlin_eval()",
      or at parse time with the \c kotlin-eval module command. Scripting requires the Kotlin scripting
      JARs, which can be fetched with \c download-kotlin-scripting-jars. Check availability with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_available() "kotlin_scripting_available()" and retry
      initialization after adding JARs with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_retry() "kotlin_scripting_retry()".
    - Kotlin and Java maps and lists participate in automatic %Qore collection conversion.
      \c java.time.LocalDateTime and \c java.time.Instant also convert to %Qore dates, preserving local
      date-time and UTC semantics respectively. See @ref jni_typesguide "type conversions".

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_providers Data Provider Integrations

    - <b>Messaging and industrial connectivity:</b>
      @ref jakartajmsdataproviderintro "JakartaJmsDataProvider" adds Jakarta JMS 3.x messaging, including
      send, request/reply, queue browsing, event-driven consumption, selectors, and durable subscriptions.
      @ref opcuadataproviderintro "OpcUaDataProvider" adds OPC UA browsing, reads, writes, method calls,
      subscriptions, security configuration, and offline NodeSet2 import/export.
    - <b>Database and application integration:</b>
      @ref jdbcdataproviderintro "JdbcDataProvider" adds database access and schema introspection with
      configurable driver JARs; @ref flywaydataproviderintro "FlywayDataProvider" manages database
      migrations; @ref cameldataproviderintro "CamelDataProvider" exposes Apache Camel integrations;
      @ref accessdataproviderintro "AccessDataProvider" reads Microsoft Access databases.
    - <b>Documents and reports:</b> new providers support
      @ref avrodataproviderintro "Avro", @ref odsdataproviderintro "ODS",
      @ref odtdataproviderintro "ODT", @ref odpdataproviderintro "ODP",
      @ref worddataproviderintro "Word", @ref powerpointdataproviderintro "PowerPoint",
      @ref vcfdataproviderintro "vCard", @ref icsdataproviderintro "iCalendar", and
      @ref emaildataproviderintro "email" formats. Document-writing actions can return generated data
      or write a file, including new writing actions in @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider".
      @ref jasperreportsdataproviderintro "JasperReportsDataProvider" generates reports;
      @ref tikadataproviderintro "TikaDataProvider" extracts content, and
      @ref visiodataproviderintro "VisioDataProvider" reads Visio documents. Document-writing actions
      require DataProvider 3.9 or later.
    - <b>Existing provider enhancements:</b> @ref kafkadataproviderintro "KafkaDataProvider" supports
      record headers and producer requests without keys. @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider"
      and @ref odsdataproviderintro "OdsDataProvider" add the \c ignore_empty read option to skip empty rows,
      and read records one row at a time from a temporary copy of their input, so .xlsx and ODS sheets with
      millions of rows are read in bounded memory instead of loading the whole workbook. Their writers add the
      \c column_formats option, a display format code for each column (such as \c dd.mm.yyyy or
      <tt>#,##0.00</tt>), so the cells keep their dates and numbers and are shown as intended in Excel and
      LibreOffice, and the \c column_masks option, the display format of each column as a mask of %Qore's
      \c ValueMask module (such as \c DD.MM.YYYY or <tt>#.##0,00 €</tt>), the masks that CSV files and Qorus
      sheet contracts are read and written with; the \c column_headers option gives header text that is not the
      column name, \c write_headers set to \c false writes no header row, and the \c timezone option gives the
      time zone whose wall-clock time dates are written in.
    - <b>Behavior change: Excel cells now hold a date's own wall-clock time, as ODS cells do.</b> The
      @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider" writer wrote a date as the wall-clock time of the Java
      virtual machine's time zone.  This affects files written by development builds of the writer, which is new
      in this release, from dates in another time zone than the Java virtual machine's (for example a date with a
      UTC offset from an API, written by a server in another time zone); dates in the server's own time zone are
      written as before.  Use the \c timezone option to write dates in a given time zone.

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_columnar Columnar JDBC Results

    - The @ref jnijdbc_driverguide "jdbc DBI driver" supports columnar queries and fetches, with dense
      nullable buffers for supported column types and list storage for driver-specific values.
      Packed decimal and temporal columns retain their precision and schema metadata. Batch extraction
      reduces per-cell JNI calls; see @ref jdbc_columnar_results "columnar result support" for the
      supported types and storage rules.
    - Java callers can use @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.selectColumnar(),
      @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.vselectColumnar(), and
      @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractSQLStatement.fetchColumnar().

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_java_api Java API Generation and Interoperability

    - Generated Java APIs preserve %Qore generic parent, interface, return, and parameter types when
      the %Qore generic-class API is available. See @ref jni_dynamic_import_generics "generic signatures".
    - Module-owned classes share one Java class identity across consuming Programs. Applications can
      also opt a non-module API class into shared identity with \c register-shared-class before loading
      it in sibling Program classloaders. See @ref jni_classloader "classloader identity and registration".
    - A %Qore method overriding or implementing a Java method is generated with the Java method's signature, and
      its return value is converted to the Java method's return type, so that Java calls it for methods with list,
      map, or numeric types that differ from the %Qore types (for example a Java \c ArrayList or \c int return
      type). See @ref jni_collection_overrides.
    - The Java method called for overloads with the same %Qore signature is chosen deterministically, as Java
      would choose it for the Java object that a %Qore list or hash is converted to; a Java object argument is
      passed to the method of its class rather than of a superclass or interface. See
      @ref jni_collection_overloads.
    - A Java class that cannot be loaded for a member's signature is declared as \c auto and reported once per class
      with the underlying Java exception, only for members that %Qore code can call (public, protected and
      package-private members).  The types of private members are declared as \c auto without importing the
      classes they name, so a library whose optional dependency is used only internally is imported without a
      report (the SQL Server JDBC driver without antlr no longer reports \c SQLServerFMTQuery), and importing a
      class creates fewer %Qore classes.  See @ref jni_member_types "declared types of Java members".
    - The bundled Byte Buddy library is updated from 1.14.19 to 1.18.10, adding support for newer Java
      class-file versions and bytecode-based superclass descriptions.

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_runtime Runtime and Resource Management

    - Added interruptible I/O support so sandboxed programs can terminate gracefully, with interrupt
      checks before JVM thread attachment; see @ref sandbox_interrupt "safe code interruption".
    - Native access for unnamed modules is enabled by default when JNI starts the JVM. The
      \c QORE_JNI_DISABLE_NATIVE_ACCESS environment variable disables this option, and
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::native_access_option_enabled() "native_access_option_enabled()" reports
      its status. See @ref jniinitguide "JVM initialization".
    - Native wrappers support automatic cleanup and safe repeated explicit release;
      see @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.release() and @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.destroy().
      @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler now implements \c AutoCloseable for use with Java
      try-with-resources; see @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler.close().
    - @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.getValidPtr() validates the backing Program;
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearContext() and
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearAllCaches() provide explicit context and cache cleanup.
    - Java provider dependency profiles validate JAR checksums, classpath contents, service entries,
      logging-provider compatibility, and logging bridge cycles in source, AOT, and installed builds.

    @subsection jni_3_0_0_bugfixes Bugfixes

    @par Java imports and class generation

    - Fixed deadlocks during concurrent Java imports, module loading, and dynamic bytecode generation.
      Fixed incorrect binding to a same-named class in another Program, missing classes from private
      module dependencies, mutually dependent class-generation failures, and inherited module-class
      lookup. See @ref jni_dynamic_import_in_java and @ref jni_classloader.
    - Fixed callback arguments resolving to the wrong %Qore class and callbacks failing when invoked
      through module method references. Callback dispatch now retains the owning Program context;
      see @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::implement_interface() "implement_interface()".
    - Fixed abstract-method resolution for Java classes imported through Python, fallback classpath
      handling when the parent loader is not a %Qore classloader, and cache invalidation in
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearCompilationCache().
    - Fixed Java compiler diagnostics being written only to standard error instead of being included
      in the exception; see @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler.
    - Fixed a %Qore class implementing a Java interface imported from the JDK or a JAR failing when its Java class
      was generated, because \c java.lang.Object was taken as its Java base class, or because a class was generated
      for the interface itself instead of using it (for nested interfaces and JDK packages).

    @par Type conversions and fields

    - Fixed Java \c java.time.OffsetDateTime, \c java.time.OffsetTime, \c java.time.LocalDate and
      \c java.time.LocalTime values being returned as Java objects instead of dates, for example the
      <tt>TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE</tt> values of the Firebird JDBC driver and the \c java.time values that JDBC 4.2
      drivers return: an \c OffsetDateTime keeps its offset, and a time of day is a time on \c 1970-01-01, as for a
      \c java.sql.Time.  The members of the Java date and time classes are declared as dates, and a date passed for
      a parameter or field of one of them is converted to its class; before, a date could only be passed for a
      \c java.time.ZonedDateTime.  The \c jdbc driver describes such columns as dates.  See
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes".

    - Mutable Java static fields now stay synchronized with their %Qore static-variable mappings.
      Map and list fields accept both the Java object and its compatible %Qore representation;
      see @ref jni_class_fields "Java field mappings".
    - Fixed the JVM crashing when a callback implementing a Java interface method with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" returned a hash.
    - A %Qore list or hash given for a list or map parameter whose class has no public constructor without
      arguments raises an exception instead of passing an object of another class to Java.
    - Fixed %Qore lists being rejected for Java collection parameters and fields. Empty lists remain
      empty arrays rather than becoming \c null, and empty trailing varargs are accepted in Debug
      builds. See @ref jni_typesguide and @ref jni_array_varargs.
    - Fixed duplicate JNI reference deletion in array construction and crashes during recursive
      class generation. Java exception conversion preserves the original exception if reading its
      metadata fails, and Java native stack frames no longer abort Debug %Qore;
      see @ref jni_exceptions "Java exceptions in Qore".
    - A \c java.time.ZonedDateTime at a whole minute keeps its time zone when converted to a %Qore date;
      before, it was read in the local time zone. This also corrects the date and time cells of the
      @ref exceldataproviderintro "Excel" and @ref odsdataproviderintro "ODS" readers with the
      \c timezone option, which are now the clock time written in the file, in that zone.

    @par Method invocation

    - Fixed \c IllegalAccessException when calling a public method of an object whose implementation class
      is not public and is in a closed module, such as \c size() on the unmodifiable set of a Kafka
      consumer's partition assignment; the method is now invoked through its accessible public interface or
      superclass declaration, without \c --add-opens. Virtual dispatch, private access restrictions, target
      exceptions, and the context class loader are preserved, and the declaration is resolved once per
      method.
    - Fixed \c INVALD-STACK-FRAME errors when %Qore code called from Java reads the frames of its call stack
      one at a time, as \c QUnit does when a Java program creates a test object: a read that stopped at a
      Java frame left the next read with fewer frames.

    - Fixed the process crashing (an assertion failing in Debug builds) when a thread's first call into Java was
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::load_class() "load_class()" for a class already imported, or
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::new_array() "new_array()": neither attached the thread to the JVM.

    @par Lifetimes and shutdown

    - Fixed crashes from expired Program bindings, callback metadata, and incomplete Java wrapper
      construction. Generated API classes reject stale native bindings instead of dereferencing
      invalid pointers; see @ref jni_dynamic_import_in_java for binding lifetime requirements.
    - Fixed leaks of callback return values and temporary class wrappers, and ensured that outstanding
      native wrappers are released during JVM shutdown. Cleanup preserves errors for explicit callers
      without propagating destructor exceptions through JNI; see
      @ref jni_qore_object_lifecycle_managementguide "object lifecycle management".
    - Fixed hangs and aborts during JVM-owned process shutdown, dynamic-class teardown, and explicit
      %Qore process exit, including the macOS shutdown failure.
    - Fixed the JVM failing to start (a crash on Linux) when \c QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS was set to an empty value or
      contained repeated, leading or trailing spaces.
    - Fixed the process aborting when a Program's Java context could not be created, for example because
      a Java class of the module's dynamic API failed to initialize; loading the module, a
      <tt>%module-cmd(jni)</tt> command, or a \c jni import now fails with the Java exception instead.
    - A Program that is being deleted no longer gets a Java context: creating one there fails with an
      exception instead of changing the Program.

    @par JDBC and data providers

    - Fixed pooled JDBC reads retaining transactions, cursors retaining invalid local JNI references,
      and incomplete statement cleanup. Fixed batch update counts, empty batches, and statement reuse
      after failed binds. Unknown batch counts return \c -1; overflow raises \c JDBC-BATCH-COUNT-ERROR.
      See @ref jdbc_transaction_boundaries "JDBC transaction boundaries".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver not executing a statement after reconnecting a lost connection: a write returned
      \c -1 without being executed, and a query returned \c -1 instead of its rows.  The statement is now executed
      again only if it cannot have taken effect (it started a transaction, which the server rolls back); a statement
      in auto-commit mode is never executed twice, and the error is raised instead.  A connection found lost when a
      transaction is started, before the statement is sent (as with the SQL Server driver), is re-established, and
      the statement is executed on the new connection.  A lost transaction raises
      \c JDBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR (it was \c ODBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR), and a \c SQLStatement released by a lost
      connection is prepared again when it is next executed.  See @ref jdbc_lost_connections "lost connections".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver ignoring every result after the first: for SQL returning several results, such as a
      batch of statements or a procedure returning several result sets, later result sets and update counts were
      lost, and so were the errors of later statements when the JDBC driver reports them only when their result is
      reached (as the SQL Server driver does).  \c exec(), \c execRaw() and \c select() now return every result,
      in the format of the \c freetds driver; \c selectRow(), \c selectRows(), \c selectColumnar() and
      \c SQLStatement skip update counts and raise an exception for more than one result set; and
      \c SQLStatement::affectedRows() returns the total of the update counts.  A statement with a single result
      returns it as before.  See @ref jdbc_multiple_results "statements with several results".
    - Fixed @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() "SQLStatement::describe()" returning nothing with the \c jdbc
      driver: it describes the columns of the result set as the other DBI drivers do (\c name, \c type, \c maxsize,
      \c native_type, \c internal_id, \c nullable, and \c precision and \c scale for decimals), with \c type
      giving the type of the values returned; the last result set is described after its last row, and SQL without
      a result set is described with an empty hash.  Code that describes statements, such as \c TableMapper's
      statement mappers, gets the columns of \c jdbc statements.  See
      @ref jdbc_statement_describe "describing statement results".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver naming a duplicate column of a result with the column's original name and a suffix,
      so that its key could differ in case from the others (ex: \c id and \c ID_1 for the columns \c Id and
      \c ID); a duplicate is named with the lower-case name and a suffix, as every column is (\c id and \c id_1).
    - Fixed @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider" reading without header options returning
      no data and rejecting cell ranges past column \c Z.
    - Fixed @ref mqttdataproviderintro "MqttDataProvider" failing to load because the required Paho
      MQTTv5 common JAR was missing.

    @par Build and documentation

    - Fixed tracking of generated Java inner-class dependencies in CMake builds.
    - Corrected Java wrapper and provider API links, parameter descriptions, and JMS tables.
      Documentation builds can reject unresolved references with <tt>-DQORE_JNI_STRICT_DOCS=ON</tt>.

